Then a great wave came up which scattered them all over the place. One of them was drowned. But the young men from Egulac did not give up the hunt. The next day they went out again and this time they took a canoe with them. They paddled out to the middle of the lake and waited. Soon they saw a large number of seals coming towards them. The young men shot at the seals with their rifles and soon there were seals floating on the water. They gathered as many as they could and put them in the canoe. They were just about to turn around and go back to the mainland when a large wave came up and sank the canoe. The young men from Egulac were all drowned. Soon afterwards a great storm hit the village of Egulac, breaking up houses and causing great damage. The people of Egulac went out and collected what they could salvage from the wreckage and then they went and buried the young men who had died in the storm.

This is a story that was told to me by an Ojibwe elder. It illustrates the idea of the supernatural caused by the actions of men. In this story, the men went out onto the lake to hunt seals, which is permitted by the laws of the harvest, but they did not show respect for the laws of the spirits. By taking a canoe onto the lake during a storm, they were disobeying the laws of the spirits, which led to their deaths. The story also illustrates the idea of the interconnectedness of all things, as the actions of the hunters led to the destruction of their village.


