They heard a voice calling them from the shore, and when they looked they saw a woman coming out from the water, and she said, "The river is rising, and if you want to save your lives, you must build a dam at the mouth of the river." So the two young men started to build a dam, and they finished it just as the river was about to overflow its banks. The woman disappeared then, and the two men went home to Egulac and told everyone what had happened, but they were not believed. Soon afterwards, during a heavy rainstorm, the river did rise and a great many people were saved because of the dam. After that, whenever there was a threat of flood, the people of Egulac would build a dam at the mouth of the river, and no one knew where the woman came from or why she had done this thing, but everyone was grateful.

This story is from the Tlingit people. What happened (in detail)? [/INST] The answer is: Similar stories can be found in other Northwest Native American cultures as well, such as the story of the Water Woman at Rock Port, Missouri, told by the Wyandotte tribe.


