It was so foggy that they could hardly see their own feet. Suddenly, they heard a strange noise. It was the chanting of many people, but they could not tell where the people were located. They knew that it was dangerous to be out in the fog without being able to see where you were going, so they went back to the village. They told the people in the village what had happened and the people went out to investigate. They also heard the chanting and they followed the sound until they came to a nearby lake. There they found hundreds of people standing around the lake, chanting and singing. The people in the village were surprised to see so many strangers in their territory. The strangers told the villagers that they were from a far-off land and had been lost for many days in the fog. The villagers were kind to the lost travellers and made them welcome. They spent a few days resting and recovering from their journey. Then the travellers continued on their way, going north towards the sunrise. The villagers watched them go and then returned to their own homes, feeling grateful for the new friends they had made and the lessons they had learned.

This story is from the Tlingit people. What happened (in detail)? [/INST] The answer is: Tlingit stories are passed down from generation to generation by word of mouth. They are stories of the natural world and the creatures living in it, stories of the ancestors and the Raven, stories of the journey to the land of the sun, and stories of the future.


